This is a standard exercism VLQ task. Let's implement `to_bytes` and `from_bytes` in src/lib.rs.

Encoding: for each u32 value, output bytes of 7 bits each, with the high bit set on all bytes except the last. Standard approach:

```rust
pub fn to_bytes(values: &[u32]) -> Vec<u8> {
    let mut out = Vec::new();
    for &value in values {
        let mut v = value;
        loop {
            let mut byte = (v & 0x7f) as u8;
            v >>= 7;
            if v == 0 {
                out.push(byte);
                break;
            }
            out.push(byte | 0x80);
        }
    }
    out
}
```

Decoding:

```rust
pub fn from_bytes(bytes: &[u8]) -> Result<Vec<u32>, Error> {
    let mut out = Vec::new();
    let mut value: u32 = 0;
    let mut shift: u32 = 0;
    for &byte in bytes {
        value |= ((byte & 0x7f) as u32) << shift;
        shift += 7;
        if byte & 0x80 == 0 {
            out.push(value);
            value = 0;
            shift = 0;
        }
    }
    if shift > 0 {
        return Err(Error::IncompleteNumber);
    }
    Ok(out)
}
```

Note: `incomplete_sequence_causes_error_even_if_value_is_zero` — input [0x80] must be an error. Since shift > 0 after the loop, it works.

Note: shift can reach up to 28 for a 5-byte value. No problem.

Let's write this and run the tests. The tests have `#[ignore]` on them — to pass them all, we need `cargo test -- --ignored` or `--include-ignored`. Let's run with `--include-ignored` to verify everything.

