16384 = 0x4000. In binary: 0100 0000 0000 0000. 7-bit groups: low 7 bits = 0000000, next 7 bits = 0000000, next = 0000100 (4). So the groups are 4, 0, 0. Big-endian VLQ: first byte = 4 | 0x80 = 0x84? Hmm, wait.

Wait, let me recalculate. 16384 = 2^14. Binary: bit 14 is set. 7-bit groups: bits 0-6 = 0, bits 7-13 = 0, bits 14-20 = 2^14 >> 14 = 1. Wait, 2^14 = 16384. Bits 14-20: 16384 >> 14 = 1. So the groups are (from MSB): 1, 0, 0. First byte = 1|0x80 = 0x81, then 0x80, then 0x00. So the expected output is [0x81, 0x80, 0x00].

My code: v = 16384. First iteration: byte = v & 0x7f = 0, v >>= 7 → 128. v != 0 so push 0|0x80 = 0x80. Second iteration: byte = 128 & 0x7f = 0, v >>= 7 → 1. Push 0x80. Third: byte = 1, v = 0, push 0x01. So the output is [0x80, 0x80, 0x01] — little-endian order!

The expected output is [0x81, 0x80, 0x00], which is big-endian: the most significant group comes first. So the byte order is: the highest 7-bit group first, with the continuation bit set on all bytes except the last.

Wait, but the spec says: "To indicate which is the last byte of the series, you leave bit #7 clear. In all of the preceding bytes, you set bit #7." So the continuation bit is set on the preceding bytes, and the last byte has bit 7 clear. And the examples: 00000080 (128) → 81 00. 128 = 0b10000000. Groups: low 7 bits = 0, next group = 1. So the order is: 1 (with continuation), then 0 (final). So the most significant group comes first. This is big-endian.

My implementation was outputting the least significant group first. I need to fix it: either collect the groups and then reverse them, or compute the number of groups first.

